1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:06,912
So far, everything ran on our laptop, in the dev target.

2
00:00:07,192 --> 00:00:10,698
Production should be built somewhere clean, only from what is in git.

3
00:00:11,648 --> 00:00:12,898
That is what CI does.

4
00:00:13,178 --> 00:00:22,698
On every push, GitHub Actions starts a fresh machine, installs dbt, downloads the data, runs dbt build, and publishes the warehouse.

5
00:00:23,648 --> 00:00:24,833
Open the project.

6
00:00:25,113 --> 00:00:31,074
First, requirements dot text in the project root: the same dbt versions Studio uses.

7
00:00:37,945 --> 00:00:46,324
Next, a folder called c i, with its own profiles dot yml: a prod target, and the database path from an environment variable.

8
00:00:51,517 --> 00:00:58,714
Type the profile: nyc taxi, target prod, type duckdb, and the path from env var duckdb path.

9
00:00:59,664 --> 00:01:03,883
The parquet files are not in git, so CI has to get the data itself.

10
00:01:04,163 --> 00:01:07,109
A folder called scripts, and download data dot py.

11
00:01:13,854 --> 00:01:14,803
Paste the script.

12
00:01:15,083 --> 00:01:24,761
It downloads the twelve files into data raw, then loads them into raw green trips in the DuckDB file, the same load we did by hand in the SQL Editor.

13
00:01:25,041 --> 00:01:25,641
Save.

14
00:01:26,591 --> 00:01:27,426
And the workflow.

15
00:01:27,706 --> 00:01:30,442
GitHub looks for it in dot github, workflows.

16
00:01:30,722 --> 00:01:34,392
Make both folders, and a file: dbt build dot yml.

17
00:01:47,141 --> 00:01:48,700
Paste the workflow, and save.

18
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:53,635
It runs on every push to main, every Monday morning, or by hand.

19
00:01:53,915 --> 00:01:55,158
It may write releases.

20
00:01:56,108 --> 00:02:09,268
The steps: check out the code, set up Python, install dbt, download and load the data, run dbt build with the c i profile, and upload prod dot duckdb to a release called warehouse.

21
00:02:10,218 --> 00:02:12,389
Open the Git tab: four new files.

22
00:02:15,153 --> 00:02:19,916
Stage all, type a message, add CI: build, test and publish the warehouse.

23
00:02:20,196 --> 00:02:20,761
Commit.

24
00:02:23,610 --> 00:02:24,585
Sync Changes.

25
00:02:24,865 --> 00:02:26,468
This push starts the first run.

26
00:02:28,710 --> 00:02:30,810
On GitHub, open the Actions tab.

27
00:02:31,090 --> 00:02:32,612
The run for our commit is there.

28
00:02:33,562 --> 00:02:34,731
Open it, and wait.

29
00:02:35,011 --> 00:02:40,706
A fresh machine installs dbt, downloads and loads the files, and builds the project.

30
00:02:40,986 --> 00:02:42,744
Under a minute later: success.

31
00:02:48,865 --> 00:02:55,403
And under Releases, the warehouse release, with prod dot duckdb attached: the whole warehouse, built by CI.

32
00:02:56,353 --> 00:03:01,077
To recap: a prod profile, pinned versions, a download script, and one workflow.

33
00:03:01,357 --> 00:03:07,085
From now on, every push builds and tests the project on a clean machine, and publishes the warehouse.

34
00:03:07,365 --> 00:03:08,909
Next, we make it a showcase.
